+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+Tasks are stored in a `Map` keyed by title in `createTaskList`; `createTask` in `task.ts` is pure. The domain may not read the clock or make random values.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+- Unique, stable ids, generated deterministically.
+- Lookup by id, and id accepted by the title-taking methods.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+- Persistence of ids, or ids unique across lists.
+- Changing how titles work as keys.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- **Counter-based ids.** Each list keeps a counter; the n-th added task gets id `task-n`. No clock, no randomness; the counter is never decremented, so ids are not reused after `remove`. Alternative (random UUID) is forbidden by the project rules.
+- **`createTask(input, id)`** takes the id from its caller (the list), keeping `task.ts` pure. `renameTask`, `addTag` etc. use object spread and keep `id`.
+- **Resolution.** The list's internal lookup tries the title first, then the id. This keeps existing behaviour for every title, and means a task whose title looks like another's id is still reached by title. `get(id)` matches ids only.
+- **Storage.** Keep the title-keyed map; resolve an id by scanning values (lists are small, in memory). `rename`'s duplicate check compares the new title to other tasks' titles after resolving the target task, so renaming by id to its own title is fine.
+- `get` returns `err('not-found', ...)`; no new error code.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- A title equal to another task's id is ambiguous; title wins, documented in the spec. The id is always reachable via `get`.
+- Id scan is O(n); acceptable for an in-memory list.
